libtls, imported unchanged from a LibreSSL release.

    version:  4.3.2
    source:   https://cdn.openbsd.org/pub/OpenBSD/LibreSSL/libressl-4.3.2.tar.gz
    files:    tls/*.[ch], tls/VERSION, tls/tls.sym, include/tls.h

This branch holds the import and nothing else. Every file here carries
the ISC notice of its OpenBSD authors, and none of them is edited on
this branch.

The port to other TLS libraries lives on the working branch, as commits
on top of a merge from here. To take a newer libtls: check this branch
out, replace these files from the new release, commit, and merge the
branch into the working branch. What conflicts is then the port against
what upstream changed, which is the question worth reading.

Two functions of OpenBSD's C library come with it, from the same
release, also unchanged:

    compat/freezero.c            crypto/compat/freezero.c
    compat/timingsafe_memcmp.c   crypto/compat/timingsafe_memcmp.c

libtls calls both. timingsafe_memcmp compares a MAC, so it is taken
rather than written again.
